==English==

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:bewteful<ety:af<beaute><-ful>>|:afeq|beauty|-ful<id:adjective>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|bewteful}}, {{m|enm|beautefull||attractive to the eye, beautiful}}, equivalent to {{suf|en|beauty|ful|pos=adjective}}. In this sense, largely displaced {{noncog|ang|fæġer}} (whence {{m|en|fair}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|byo͞oʹtĭ-fəl|a=UK}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈbjuːtɪ.fəl/|/ˈbjuːtɪ.fʊl/}}
* {{audio|en|En-uk-beautiful.ogg|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbju.tɪ.fəl/|[bju.ɾə.fəl]|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-beautiful.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyphenation|en|beau|ti|ful}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Possessing [[beauty]], impressing the [[eye]]; [[attractive]]. {{defdate|from 1520s}}
#: {{ux|en|Anyone who has ever met her thought she was absolutely '''beautiful'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|There's a '''beautiful''' lake by the town.}}
#* {{RQ:Drayton Poly-Olbion|part=2|chapter=The Sixe and Twentieth Song|page=124|passage=[A]s amongſt the ''Moores'', the Iettieſt blacke are deem'd / The '''beautifulſt''' of them; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author={{w|Edgar Rice Burroughs}}|title={{w|The Return of Tarzan}}|publisher=Ballantine Books|location=New York|year_published=1963|page=214|passage=“Is Olga de Coude very '''beautiful'''?” she asked. And Tarzan laughed and kissed her again. “Not one-tenth so '''beautiful''' as you, dear,” he said.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=5|passage=It's rather like a '''beautiful''' Inverness cloak one has inherited. Much too good to hide away, so one wears it instead of an overcoat and pretends it's an amusing new fashion.}}
#* {{quote-av|1=en|year=2016|url=https://learningenglish.voanews.com/a/lets-learn-english-lesson-5-where-are-you/3168971.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170930001420/https://learningenglish.voanews.com/a/lets-learn-english-lesson-5-where-are-you/3168971.html|archivedate=30 September 2017|title=VOA Learning English (public domain)|text=It is a '''beautiful''' kitchen! &mdash; It ''is'' '''beautiful'''.}}
## {{n-g|Used emphatically or ironically, after the noun it qualifies.}} {{defdate|from 1850s}}
##: {{ux|en|the house '''beautiful'''}}
# That one [[like]]s very much.
#: {{ux|en|He was a '''beautiful''' person; he would drop everything to help you.}}
#: {{ux|en|You've done a '''beautiful''' thing today.}}
# Affording [[pleasure]] to any of the [[sense]]s, especially that of [[hearing]]; [[delightful]]. {{defdate|from 1860s}}
#: {{ux|en|Beethoven's most '''beautiful''' sonata}}
#: {{ux|en|It's '''beautiful''' outside, let's go for a walk.}}
#: {{ux|en|These carnations smell '''beautiful''' but those daffodils smell funny.}}
# Impressing with [[charm]] in an [[intellectual]] or [[moral]] way, through inherent [[suitability]] or [[elegance]]. {{defdate|from 1580s}}
#: {{ux|en|The skater performed a '''beautiful''' [[axel]].}}

====Usage notes====
* When used to refer to human appearance, the word is more commonly used for women, with {{m|en|handsome}} being more common for men, though neither is incorrect. For a man, ''beautiful'' could connote a more delicate or androgynous appearance.
* The comparatives '''[[beautifuler]]''' and '''[[beautifuller]]''' and the superlatives '''[[beautifulest]]''' and '''[[beautifullest]]''' have also occasionally been used, but they are nonstandard.

